WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.837
This lesson is all about turning raw agent interactions

2
00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.638
into durable knowledge.

3
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:10.043
You'll build pipelines that extract structured facts from conversations,

4
00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:14.047
consolidate episodic memory into semantic memory.

5
00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:17.083
and create write-back loops that let your agent

6
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.587
update and refine its own memory autonomously.

7
00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:24.090
Let's get coding. In this lesson,

8
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.093
we will do an overview of the most common memory operations.

9
00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:30.096
We will also talk about Context Engineering

10
00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.098
and how to use it to our advantage.

11
00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:36.102
how to efficiently use context window reduction techniques.

12
00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:40.106
And finally, we will talk about something called Workflow memory

13
00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:44.310
and how to summarize efficiently to improve our memory operations.

14
00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:47.313
So the most common memory operations,

15
00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:49.315
which we have already seen in lesson two.

16
00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.618
First, we have the conversational memory,

17
00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:54.287
which is typically stored in an SQL Table

18
00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.990
because it's conversational, so we want to store

19
00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.992
a lot of it in raw text.

20
00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:00.794
And the rest of the operations,

21
00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:02.996
for instance, the Knowledge Base,

22
00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:06.066
the workflow, or the toolbox patterns,

23
00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:08.268
the entity and the summaries

24
00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:11.071
are usually saved as vectors because

25
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:14.774
they are very helpful to be stored in this type

26
00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.077
as we want to perform similarity search

27
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:18.778
in one way or the other.

28
00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:21.781
As we saw in the last lesson with the toolbox pattern.

29
00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:24.017
So, what is context engineering?

30
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.319
Context engineering is a technique

31
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.822
where we optimally and carefully select

32
00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.924
the amount of information that goes

33
00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.626
into a large language model's context window

34
00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:37.330
by shaping the amount of information that we put there.

35
00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:41.601
This helps us make the large language model responses much more efficient

36
00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.904
or tailored to the actual input from the user,

37
00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:49.209
and this will actually produce better responses by the large language model.

38
00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:52.412
In the real world, we have lots of data sources.

39
00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:55.415
We have access to databases, APIs,

40
00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:58.518
MCP servers, and also the internet.

41
00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:03.423
So these are all potential context window components and contents

42
00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:07.527
that can go into the actual context window.

43
00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:11.965
But we cannot put all of it into the context window

44
00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.400
as it's a limited thing. So

45
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.669
we need to carefully and optimally select

46
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.272
the context that actually will go

47
00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.374
into the LLM context window.

48
00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:24.377
So what do we do if we have more information

49
00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.679
than what the context window allows?

50
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.283
So for this, we will talk about Context Window Reduction,

51
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.887
which is the process of reducing or shrinking the information

52
00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.489
that is actually placed on it.

53
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:41.394
There are two main techniques, Context Summarization and Context Compaction.

54
00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.796
Each of these has their own advantages

55
00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:46.199
and its disadvantages, which we will see now.

56
00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:50.003
Context Summarization is the process of taking the whole context

57
00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.405
of the large language model

58
00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:55.108
and running it through the LLM

59
00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:58.511
where we want to focus on three things.

60
00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:02.348
So first, we want to retain the highest-signal

61
00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.618
information from the context, removing the low-signal parts

62
00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:08.621
and retaining the task-relevant facts

63
00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:12.625
and claims about it. Secondly,

64
00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.628
we want to preserve the meaning and the key relationships

65
00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:19.132
in the conversations so that if we

66
00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:23.336
remove a part of it, we don't lose track of the actual

67
00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:25.538
purpose of the prompt of the user.

68
00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:28.541
And thirdly, we want to remove redundant parts

69
00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.443
and also useless parts.

70
00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:32.745
what you might consider a useless part,

71
00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.747
something that has low-value detail

72
00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:37.550
or something that is not directly connected

73
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.552
to the actual query of the user.

74
00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:42.755
And after performing Context Summarization,

75
00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.025
you will get a smaller input so that the large language model

76
00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.528
can better focus on the task at hand.

77
00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:52.732
The definition of Context summarization is the process of compressing

78
00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:56.436
all this context into a shorter representation

79
00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:59.439
that preserves the best information

80
00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:02.742
and the highest and most task-relevant

81
00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:04.844
data from the original.

82
00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.513
So Context Summarization is one of the

83
00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.083
two techniques that we have at our disposal

84
00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:15.083
where we inject the summarized context into a clean context window

85
00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:17.790
by wiping the previous conversation with the LLM,

86
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.262
so that the final response can actually make the large language model

87
00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:26.065
reason everything again from the summary as a starting point.

88
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.535
But this has one disadvantage, which is

89
00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:32.138
this will always be a lossy technique.

90
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:35.508
What do we mean by that? A lossy technique means

91
00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:38.211
that we will always have some data loss.

92
00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:42.215
We will always lose a little bit of information in the process.

93
00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:45.918
And then we have Context Compaction, which is our second technique.

94
00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:50.290
And with Context Compaction, rather than summarizing the information,

95
00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.792
we will just transfer it into the database

96
00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:56.296
and use the database as an external addition

97
00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:59.399
to the large language model, so that whenever the large language model

98
00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:02.302
needs more information about it,

99
00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:06.005
can just go into the database and access this information.

100
00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:10.310
So this allows us to offload a lot of the context size

101
00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.713
into the database and let the large language model decide

102
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.383
when it wants to access it. How do we do that?

103
00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.919
We just need to get this information,

104
00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:23.423
put it with an ID into the database,

105
00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:26.225
and then let the large language model know

106
00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.928
that this information is available just in case

107
00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:33.232
and we can complement this identifier with a description

108
00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:36.836
of the chunk that we just took out of the context.

109
00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:40.840
So that if the large language model is only interested

110
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.545
in a very general idea of what the compacted content was,

111
00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:47.847
it can just take a look at the description.

112
00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.649
And if it needs more information,

113
00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.584
it can just go into the database

114
00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.020
and actually pull the whole context out.

115
00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:56.556
What about Workflow Memory?

116
00:05:56.556 --> 00:06:01.260
Workflow Memory is a technique which is also aided by a database

117
00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:03.896
where, for instance, going back to the

118
00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:06.366
current weather example that we had before.

119
00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.668
Imagine that the user asks,

120
00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:10.670
Get me the current weather, right?

121
00:06:10.837 --> 00:06:12.739
This is something that requires a lot

122
00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:14.640
of steps to perform by an LLM.

123
00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:20.041
So Workflow Memory will actually help us streamline and preserve a sequence

124
00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:24.350
of the required steps needed to perform this query.

125
00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.920
For instance, to get the current weather, we might want to

126
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.223
get the user location.

127
00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:32.725
Then we want to access a weather API

128
00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:35.228
or an application tool

129
00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:37.730
and pass the latitude and longitude

130
00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.732
of the user into the application.

131
00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.235
Then we will be able to get the current weather

132
00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:44.237
for those coordinates,

133
00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:47.707
and finally return the weather response to the user.

134
00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:51.310
Now, since this is a multi-step process,

135
00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.513
Workflow Memory helps us with this

136
00:06:53.513 --> 00:06:57.016
because we can reuse this set of steps

137
00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:59.719
to get the current weather for any user

138
00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:01.788
after we perform it once.

139
00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:05.658
So we can preserve the structure of work over time

140
00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:07.960
in the database in a way that makes

141
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.163
the large language model know exactly

142
00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:13.266
what steps to follow to respond to the users,

143
00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.135
we can have this structure

144
00:07:16.369 --> 00:07:18.971
and if you remember what we were talking about before,

145
00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:22.542
Workflow Memory can be used in a streamlined way

146
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:25.745
and we can use this relational table to store

147
00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.747
information about the steps,

148
00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:31.951
a timestamp of when these steps were executed

149
00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:34.754
and also an embedding of all the steps.

150
00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:37.089
This would be an example.

151
00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:39.892
You have a workflow name with an optional description

152
00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:44.096
and the original user request with a set of ordered steps

153
00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:47.800
so that the large language model is able to reproduce this

154
00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.803
over and over without having to figure out

155
00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.005
on the spot what to do.

156
00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:57.243
This will help the large language model know exactly what to do

157
00:07:57.243 --> 00:07:59.378
on each user request without having

158
00:07:59.378 --> 00:08:01.848
to figure it out on the fly.

159
00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:03.549
And as a consequence,

160
00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:07.286
this will make the large language model much more efficient

161
00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:11.123
and will reduce the amount of context required to process

162
00:08:11.123 --> 00:08:13.526
and actually respond to the user.

163
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.963
We're going to talk about memory operations,

164
00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:20.066
context engineering and the ways that we have

165
00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:22.568
to reduce the context efficiently.

166
00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:25.938
So, just like we have done in the previous lessons,

167
00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:29.041
we need to set up the access to our database.

168
00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:34.709
And as you'll see, we're correctly connected into the database again.

169
00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:38.518
We are also going to initialize our OpenAI client

170
00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:41.120
so we can use their GPT models

171
00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:43.623
as well as the embedding model

172
00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:46.325
from Hugging Face that we used before.

173
00:08:46.726 --> 00:08:49.929
We're also going to recall our table names

174
00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:53.533
so that we can properly address them in our store manager

175
00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:55.902
and our memory managers.

176
00:08:55.902 --> 00:08:59.505
And as we did before, we're also going to update

177
00:08:59.505 --> 00:09:01.908
and recreate our

178
00:09:02.275 --> 00:09:05.378
conversational history table and our tool log history table

179
00:09:05.378 --> 00:09:08.481
so we have some kind of reference system for the future

180
00:09:08.481 --> 00:09:10.483
when we inspect these logs.

181
00:09:10.483 --> 00:09:13.386
And you see here that they already exist,

182
00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:15.488
but if you were running this for the first time,

183
00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:17.456
you'll see that they were properly created.

184
00:09:17.823 --> 00:09:22.628
And after we created all our objects, our conversation tables

185
00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.831
and our vector stores, we are going to reinitialize

186
00:09:25.932 --> 00:09:29.335
our StoreManager and get all the store objects

187
00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:31.837
that we need to operate.

188
00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:34.507
as you'll see, they have all been loaded properly

189
00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.409
via the StoreManager.

190
00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:39.912
And the same goes for the instantiation

191
00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:42.515
of the MemoryManager and our Toolbox.

192
00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:44.917
So we can select the tools

193
00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:47.920
so that the large language model is able to select

194
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.522
the right tools at the right time.

195
00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:52.325
and they have been properly initialized.

196
00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:56.228
And very quickly before operating with these models,

197
00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:59.732
you're going to set a maximum number of tokens for the model,

198
00:09:59.732 --> 00:10:03.235
which in this case is going to be 256000.

199
00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:05.938
So, since the purpose of this

200
00:10:05.938 --> 00:10:08.641
chapter is to work with context sizes,

201
00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:12.812
we are going to create this function called calculate_context_usage,

202
00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:16.415
which is going to do an estimation of the amount of tokens

203
00:10:16.816 --> 00:10:19.919
that are actually present in the context at any time.

204
00:10:20.353 --> 00:10:24.357
We need this to be able to assess when to actually perform

205
00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:27.360
context summarization or context compaction,

206
00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.363
which were the two techniques that we described before.

207
00:10:30.363 --> 00:10:33.866
So here we're doing a simple estimation by dividing

208
00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:36.869
the total number of characters by four.

209
00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:39.038
You'll see that depending on the

210
00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:41.307
way that each model stores the tokens,

211
00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:43.976
you will find that some of them divide by two,

212
00:10:43.976 --> 00:10:45.578
some of them divide by four.

213
00:10:45.945 --> 00:10:49.949
But we're going to use this estimation as four characters per token.

214
00:10:50.583 --> 00:10:52.885
And then we're going to set a percentage

215
00:10:52.885 --> 00:10:54.987
so that the maximum is never reached.

216
00:10:55.488 --> 00:10:57.590
And now we're going to create

217
00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.992
our first summarization function.

218
00:11:00.793 --> 00:11:04.296
So, since summarization is one of the two techniques

219
00:11:04.296 --> 00:11:06.499
that we described in our lesson,

220
00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:09.301
we need to be able to discern

221
00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:12.405
when summarization is optimal to perform

222
00:11:12.505 --> 00:11:15.207
and what instructions we need to give to the large language model

223
00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:16.976
to perform summarization.

224
00:11:17.476 --> 00:11:19.578
You can use different prompts.

225
00:11:19.979 --> 00:11:21.747
I invite you to actually try

226
00:11:21.747 --> 00:11:23.783
different prompts for each one of these.

227
00:11:24.116 --> 00:11:28.020
In general, there are different types of prompts that work better,

228
00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.690
I guess, against some types of problems.

229
00:11:31.357 --> 00:11:33.959
But here is an example of the prompt

230
00:11:33.959 --> 00:11:35.695
that we wanted to give.

231
00:11:36.595 --> 00:11:39.999
And remember that summarization is a lossy technique,

232
00:11:39.999 --> 00:11:41.801
so we will always lose a

233
00:11:41.801 --> 00:11:43.903
little bit of information in the process.

234
00:11:44.503 --> 00:11:48.107
So the prompting technique that you use for summarization

235
00:11:48.107 --> 00:11:51.811
will determine the quality of the output that you generate.

236
00:11:52.244 --> 00:11:55.047
In this case, we're going to summarize the conversation

237
00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:58.250
so that it can be resumed in the future

238
00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:01.153
by losing the least amount of information

239
00:12:01.153 --> 00:12:05.357
and preserving the highest context signals from our context.

240
00:12:05.658 --> 00:12:07.860
So we want to preserve all the

241
00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:12.164
important information and we remove the things that the LLM will consider

242
00:12:12.164 --> 00:12:14.767
as least important in this case.

243
00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:17.837
So for that, we want to keep concrete details.

244
00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:22.074
We want to separate the facts from open-ended things

245
00:12:22.074 --> 00:12:23.776
like questions

246
00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:26.445
to not hallucinate any information

247
00:12:26.445 --> 00:12:30.149
or include any hints generated by the LLM,

248
00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:32.752
and to keep it as concise as possible

249
00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:35.254
without being useless in the future.

250
00:12:35.254 --> 00:12:37.156
So we need it to be useful too.

251
00:12:37.323 --> 00:12:41.127
and we want to split and actually preserve

252
00:12:41.527 --> 00:12:44.230
only a maximum of 6000 characters

253
00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:48.334
in our final response. So this is what we do here.

254
00:12:48.734 --> 00:12:52.238
We call the LLM client with the model that we wanted,

255
00:12:52.671 --> 00:12:56.041
with a maximum number of completion tokens for the response,

256
00:12:56.442 --> 00:13:01.442
with this structured OpenAI specification request and after that,

257
00:13:02.114 --> 00:13:06.652
we will be able to retrieve the actual summary.

258
00:13:07.319 --> 00:13:09.288
We also have this safety mechanism here

259
00:13:09.588 --> 00:13:12.625
so that if the summarization fails the first time,

260
00:13:13.025 --> 00:13:16.929
we also have a fallback mechanism that tries a simpler prompt

261
00:13:17.229 --> 00:13:20.733
without that many concepts on the prompt,

262
00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.202
so that if the first output is empty,

263
00:13:23.402 --> 00:13:25.771
we will never completely lose all

264
00:13:25.771 --> 00:13:28.507
the context from the original user's input.

265
00:13:29.108 --> 00:13:32.878
So in this case, we are reducing the amount of instructions

266
00:13:32.878 --> 00:13:35.281
that we give to the large language model.

267
00:13:35.681 --> 00:13:37.583
And as opposed to what we did,

268
00:13:37.583 --> 00:13:39.585
we also need to have a mechanism

269
00:13:39.885 --> 00:13:42.288
to expand the summary as well

270
00:13:42.588 --> 00:13:44.990
to retrieve the original conversations.

271
00:13:45.591 --> 00:13:48.894
So in case that we stored a summary identifier

272
00:13:49.395 --> 00:13:51.297
through context compaction,

273
00:13:51.697 --> 00:13:54.500
we also need to be able to do the opposite.

274
00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:57.403
And we create this expand_summary function here

275
00:13:57.403 --> 00:14:00.873
to actually perform this functionality. So for that,

276
00:14:00.873 --> 00:14:05.110
We need to obtain the summary_text from the memory_manager

277
00:14:05.177 --> 00:14:10.177
by giving it the summary_id from whatever we compact.

278
00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:14.653
And after that, we can get the original context

279
00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.957
that was previously summarized or compacted.

280
00:14:18.691 --> 00:14:23.095
And now that we have this unsummarized

281
00:14:23.095 --> 00:14:26.498
and compaction versus summarization system,

282
00:14:26.799 --> 00:14:30.803
we are going to create this function that does five steps.

283
00:14:31.337 --> 00:14:33.539
The first step is to read

284
00:14:33.539 --> 00:14:36.141
all the unsummarized messages from a thread.

285
00:14:36.342 --> 00:14:38.344
A thread meaning a conversation,

286
00:14:38.644 --> 00:14:41.347
generating a summary via the LLM,

287
00:14:41.647 --> 00:14:43.816
so performing context summarization.

288
00:14:44.149 --> 00:14:47.119
Then storing this summary in the summary memory

289
00:14:47.319 --> 00:14:50.289
and marking the actual rows that were

290
00:14:50.289 --> 00:14:53.225
used in the summarization with an identifier.

291
00:14:53.826 --> 00:14:58.130
And finally, returning the summary object for future use.

292
00:14:58.731 --> 00:15:03.731
In this case, first of all, we read the unsummarized conversation

293
00:15:04.536 --> 00:15:06.739
with this SQL query,

294
00:15:07.239 --> 00:15:09.742
which basically goes into the memory_manager,

295
00:15:09.942 --> 00:15:12.211
goes through our whole conversation table,

296
00:15:12.511 --> 00:15:16.515
which as you will remember is stored in an SQL table

297
00:15:16.715 --> 00:15:19.919
rather than vectors, so we can trace back the timestamp,

298
00:15:20.419 --> 00:15:22.922
the ID of the conversation and the chat,

299
00:15:23.222 --> 00:15:26.225
and also all the content in the conversation.

300
00:15:26.525 --> 00:15:29.028
And we are going to use this information,

301
00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:31.330
all the rows that we get from that,

302
00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.333
to build a transcript from the conversation.

303
00:15:35.334 --> 00:15:38.737
So up until this point, we're just rebuilding the conversation

304
00:15:38.737 --> 00:15:41.140
that we had with a large language model.

305
00:15:41.307 --> 00:15:46.111
Then, we're going to summarize its context window through the transcript

306
00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:49.114
and with the help of the memory_manager

307
00:15:49.114 --> 00:15:52.117
and making use of the OpenAI client.

308
00:15:52.117 --> 00:15:55.521
Then we're going to generate a summary_id for that.

309
00:15:55.521 --> 00:15:59.158
We're going to mark the rows that were used on summarization.

310
00:15:59.158 --> 00:16:03.128
So if in the future we want to perform summarization again,

311
00:16:03.128 --> 00:16:07.633
we know that this information has already previously been summarized.

312
00:16:08.133 --> 00:16:11.437
So we do some updating on the memory_manager for this.

313
00:16:11.971 --> 00:16:15.474
And finally, we will mark the messages as summarized

314
00:16:15.474 --> 00:16:18.777
and return the summarized result.

315
00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:22.781
And as we talked about on the lesson,

316
00:16:22.781 --> 00:16:25.484
we also have this function

317
00:16:25.484 --> 00:16:28.454
which is going to help us compact the information

318
00:16:28.454 --> 00:16:30.923
and put an ID and a

319
00:16:30.923 --> 00:16:33.759
summary of the conversation into the database.

320
00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:36.962
So, in this case, this function

321
00:16:37.563 --> 00:16:39.698
is going to first go into

322
00:16:39.698 --> 00:16:42.201
the whole context that we pass it,

323
00:16:42.401 --> 00:16:45.604
and through the llm_client and the memory_manager,

324
00:16:46.105 --> 00:16:50.209
obtain the thread_id or the summary ID of the conversation.

325
00:16:50.576 --> 00:16:54.380
Then it's going to replace the conversation section

326
00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:58.384
by taking the information from the identifier

327
00:16:58.684 --> 00:17:00.886
that it just got and performing

328
00:17:01.286 --> 00:17:03.889
a compaction of this context by

329
00:17:04.189 --> 00:17:06.692
rebuilding the context.

330
00:17:07.292 --> 00:17:11.096
So, after this function is executed,

331
00:17:11.096 --> 00:17:13.399
we can obtain the compacted context

332
00:17:13.399 --> 00:17:16.001
which will help the large language model

333
00:17:16.001 --> 00:17:18.904
select the actual information when it needs.

334
00:17:19.304 --> 00:17:21.407
and we run that.

335
00:17:21.607 --> 00:17:24.209
And in the hopes of implementing all the

336
00:17:24.209 --> 00:17:27.112
concepts that we are looking at in the lesson,

337
00:17:27.212 --> 00:17:31.316
we're also going to register this as a toolbox pattern,

338
00:17:31.417 --> 00:17:33.118
so as a new tool, so

339
00:17:33.118 --> 00:17:35.087
that the large language model is also

340
00:17:35.087 --> 00:17:38.290
able to perform this automatically when it needs.

341
00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:42.394
So whenever the large language model reaches 80% utilization,

342
00:17:42.795 --> 00:17:46.065
it can automatically summarize and store the conversation

343
00:17:46.565 --> 00:17:50.369
into the database, effectively performing context compaction.

344
00:17:50.836 --> 00:17:53.739
You will see that this is the importance of having

345
00:17:53.739 --> 00:17:55.941
the toolbox pattern active.

346
00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:59.645
We have been creating tens of tools

347
00:17:59.645 --> 00:18:01.647
and it would be very naive to

348
00:18:01.647 --> 00:18:03.649
actually put them all into the context

349
00:18:03.649 --> 00:18:07.853
as it would cause a lot of context bloating, context confusion.

350
00:18:08.353 --> 00:18:10.656
It would increase your latency

351
00:18:10.856 --> 00:18:14.660
and essentially confuse the whole large language model processing.

352
00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:17.329
So the toolbox pattern will help us with that.

353
00:18:17.529 --> 00:18:20.132
And we will also create this monitor_context_window

354
00:18:20.132 --> 00:18:23.535
which will warn us of the actual context

355
00:18:23.535 --> 00:18:26.438
utilization at any time

356
00:18:26.438 --> 00:18:28.841
by doing the estimations that we did before.

357
00:18:29.241 --> 00:18:33.645
So by using the function that we created earlier on the notebook,

358
00:18:33.645 --> 00:18:35.714
we're going to calculate an estimation

359
00:18:35.714 --> 00:18:38.150
of the context that we are doing

360
00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:40.419
and add basically a value that will

361
00:18:40.419 --> 00:18:43.055
tell us if we are on the green,

362
00:18:43.055 --> 00:18:46.158
if we need to worry about utilization,

363
00:18:46.158 --> 00:18:49.094
or if we are at a critical level

364
00:18:49.094 --> 00:18:52.030
where context summarization or compaction needs to happen.

365
00:18:52.865 --> 00:18:54.833
So now the testing begins.

366
00:18:55.134 --> 00:18:58.137
All the functions, the tools that we have created

367
00:18:58.137 --> 00:19:00.639
and made available to the large language model

368
00:19:00.639 --> 00:19:03.142
are going to be made available right now

369
00:19:03.142 --> 00:19:07.146
and we are going to test them by creating a sample conversation

370
00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:09.748
about a research that we want to make

371
00:19:09.948 --> 00:19:12.551
and we're going to get to see the results.

372
00:19:12.951 --> 00:19:17.723
So in this case, we have this test sampled research conversation

373
00:19:17.723 --> 00:19:21.727
where we are supposedly working on our PhD thesis

374
00:19:21.727 --> 00:19:25.731
and we were talking with our GPT model about what to do,

375
00:19:25.731 --> 00:19:29.434
what to research, some of the things about RAG.

376
00:19:29.868 --> 00:19:32.671
So we have a thread on our database

377
00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:34.873
in the conversational table

378
00:19:35.374 --> 00:19:37.376
which we can access through the memory manager

379
00:19:37.676 --> 00:19:39.678
with a total of 32 messages.

380
00:19:40.179 --> 00:19:42.447
And now we're going to actually

381
00:19:42.447 --> 00:19:45.083
monitor how much context we are using.

382
00:19:45.284 --> 00:19:48.787
So for that, we are going to call the monitor_context_window

383
00:19:48.987 --> 00:19:52.891
with the current_context, which includes the 32 messages,

384
00:19:53.091 --> 00:19:54.560
and we are going to print

385
00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:56.261
the amount of tokens that we have.

386
00:19:56.261 --> 00:19:59.364
maximum allowed by the GPT model that we're using,

387
00:19:59.364 --> 00:20:02.768
the percentage usage that we currently have,

388
00:20:02.768 --> 00:20:06.271
and the status, being okay, warning, or critical.

389
00:20:06.271 --> 00:20:10.075
Meaning, if we need to make context summarization or compaction,

390
00:20:10.075 --> 00:20:11.877
or if we are still okay.

391
00:20:12.377 --> 00:20:14.780
As you'll see here, our context window monitor

392
00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:19.184
currently says that we are using a total of 2000 tokens,

393
00:20:19.885 --> 00:20:22.387
which is less than 1% of the maximum.

394
00:20:22.588 --> 00:20:24.590
So at this point, we are fine.

395
00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:27.192
We don't need to perform context summarization

396
00:20:27.192 --> 00:20:29.861
because we still have a lot of space in our context.

397
00:20:30.162 --> 00:20:32.364
So the attention matrix of our model

398
00:20:32.864 --> 00:20:34.866
is not heavily overloaded at the moment.

399
00:20:35.167 --> 00:20:37.536
And this is actually a print of

400
00:20:37.536 --> 00:20:39.871
the current conversation with the 32 messages.

401
00:20:40.205 --> 00:20:44.042
Now, even if we haven't reached 80% utilization,

402
00:20:44.042 --> 00:20:46.778
we are going to call the summarize_conversation

403
00:20:46.778 --> 00:20:51.083
so that when you are filling up your context window,

404
00:20:51.083 --> 00:20:53.986
you can manually use the summarize_conversation

405
00:20:53.986 --> 00:20:55.887
whenever you feel like it.

406
00:20:56.388 --> 00:20:58.490
So for that, we're calling the summarize_conversation

407
00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:02.794
and it's going to call the OpenAI model and perform this.

408
00:21:03.395 --> 00:21:05.497
And as you'll see,

409
00:21:05.497 --> 00:21:07.299
we have our conversation summarized.

410
00:21:07.599 --> 00:21:10.569
Apart from the summary, we will also get an ID,

411
00:21:10.569 --> 00:21:14.473
which can be optionally used together with a description

412
00:21:14.473 --> 00:21:17.175
to store this into the database

413
00:21:17.175 --> 00:21:19.578
as a context compaction feature.

414
00:21:19.878 --> 00:21:24.283
And we also mark all the messages as summarized,

415
00:21:24.283 --> 00:21:27.085
so that whenever we want to summarize again,

416
00:21:27.319 --> 00:21:30.622
We don't perform the summarization against these messages

417
00:21:30.922 --> 00:21:33.625
as they already have a summary and a description

418
00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:37.329
explaining what these messages actually entail.

419
00:21:38.063 --> 00:21:40.299
And this is the final summary

420
00:21:40.299 --> 00:21:42.868
that was created from the 32 messages.

421
00:21:43.869 --> 00:21:46.371
And now we will do exactly the opposite.

422
00:21:46.772 --> 00:21:50.409
Instead of compacting the summary and creating a summary ID,

423
00:21:50.709 --> 00:21:52.711
we will take the ID to

424
00:21:52.711 --> 00:21:55.047
actually query the database and uncompact it

425
00:21:55.347 --> 00:21:58.216
by calling the expand_summary tool,

426
00:21:58.617 --> 00:22:01.586
as we already registered it into the toolbox.

427
00:22:02.154 --> 00:22:06.325
And as you'll see here, we have the summary ID

428
00:22:06.625 --> 00:22:09.127
and by getting the information from the database,

429
00:22:09.127 --> 00:22:12.130
we have effectively allowed the large language model

430
00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.634
to expand its memory core with the database,

431
00:22:15.834 --> 00:22:18.337
so that we can retrieve the summary context,

432
00:22:18.503 --> 00:22:21.239
a description of what was in the conversation,

433
00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:23.909
plus the original 32 messages.

434
00:22:24.376 --> 00:22:26.578
And now we just have this pipeline

435
00:22:26.578 --> 00:22:28.780
which will help us verify

436
00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:32.184
that all the summarized rows in the database are omitted

437
00:22:32.184 --> 00:22:35.187
from the summarization efforts.

438
00:22:35.721 --> 00:22:39.524
So, in this case, after having summarized a thread,

439
00:22:39.725 --> 00:22:44.229
we need to check on database that they have been marked properly.

440
00:22:44.629 --> 00:22:47.532
So we execute some SQL code

441
00:22:47.532 --> 00:22:51.536
to get the amount of rows that actually are summarized

442
00:22:51.536 --> 00:22:53.905
and which ones are not summarized.

443
00:22:54.206 --> 00:22:56.508
And we print them on these variables.

444
00:22:57.142 --> 00:23:00.445
So, as you'll see here, through our memory manager,

445
00:23:00.645 --> 00:23:03.048
we have our conversational memory and

446
00:23:03.148 --> 00:23:06.952
no unsummarized messages were found for the thread

447
00:23:06.952 --> 00:23:08.854
as we already summarized.

448
00:23:09.054 --> 00:23:11.456
So they were all marked as summarized.

449
00:23:12.157 --> 00:23:16.862
And with this, you have successfully completed lesson 4. Congratulations.
